  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustaining holistic and comprehensive approaches on HIV and AIDS responses in the Catholic Diocese</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Diocese of Moshi is one of the 30 Catholic Dioceses of Tanzania. Through Rainbow Centre, the Diocese manages a HIV and AIDs prevention, care and support services project in Moshi Municipality, Moshi Rural, Rombo, and Hai districts of Kilimanjaro region.  Rainbow Centre (RC) has gained wide experience through service provision to its target groups and also in managing emerging situations in the course of its operation.  HIV prevalence is currently high among women aged 30-34 years and men aged 40-44 years, a shift from the pattern in the early 1990s. 
This programme intends to utilize the gains made from previous programme implementation (2008-2010), lessons learnt and best practices to consciously encourage beneficiaries to utilize services while formulating strategies of caring for and supporting those within their midst.
Rainbow Centre has developed a three year plan (2011-2014), seeking to comprehensively respond to various challenges the programme continues to experience in the course of implementation.  These challenges include: issues of misconceptions based on traditions and customs which negatively impact on attitude change towards HIV and AIDS, care for Orphan and vulnerable children, access to treatment and nutritional support for PLHIV, and high poverty levels at households of infected and affected beneficiaries. 
The proposal under appraisal therefore seeks to respond to these challenges through implementing a comprehensive, holistic programme of prevention, care and support.  Through its current HIV and AIDS implementation team, RC aims to increase knowledge and awareness of HIV and AIDS and provide education on HIV infection and reproductive health through seminars and workshops.  Care, support and mitigation responses intended to improve quality of life for PLHIV will be enhanced; programme performance will be strengthened by way of zonal supervision visits and resource mobilisation scale up.   
It is envisaged that this result-oriented programme will benefit the community through the replication model of lessons learnt.  Developed expertise and experience will be transferred to other communities.  Other direct benefits of the programme will include: improved quality of life among PLHIV through IGAs, provision of basic needs and scholarly materials, and psychological support for orphans  
The primary targets of the project are PLHIV who are adult men, women, boys and girls.  Secondary target groups are people affected by HIV and AIDS including OVCs, guardians/care givers, and the community at large.  This appraisal seeks to fund Rainbow centre for three years starting April 2011, in line with the stand alone project policy.  
This project has been extended for a further one year to end in March 2014, after the end of the three year cycle covering the period April 2010 to March 2013.  During the extension period, CAFOD will support Moshi to roll out its exit strategies aimed at ensuring continuity of project activities after CAFOD&#x2019;s phase out from Tanzania.</narrative>
  </description>
